// T4 slice release, the NEGATIVE case: a SUB-SLICE must never be released.
//
// `s := ys[1:3]` gives an INTERIOR pointer into the buffer that `xs` owns in
// main. A release of it computes `interior - GOO_OBJ_HEADER_SIZE` and hands that
// to free(), which is the single most dangerous thing the slice release could do.
// `goo_obj_headerless` (src/runtime/runtime.c) catches only NULL and
// goo_zerobase, so nothing at RUNTIME would notice. The refusal is entirely
// static.
//
// Condition 2 refuses it: AST_SLICE_INDEX_EXPR is a view, not an owned value.
// Measured on this shape before the emission was written -- `s` reads
// RELEASE_NO_NOT_OWNED. release_decision_test row 7 pins the rule.
//
// main reads `xs` AFTER take has returned a thousand times, so a wrongly released
// sub-slice shows up as an invalid free and then an invalid read rather than as
// silent corruption.
//
// `xs` itself is NOT released either, and that is load-bearing for this probe:
// take's summary marks its parameter escaping, so the buffer survives for the
// final read.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record.
package main

var sink int

func take(ys []int) int {
	s := ys[1:3]
	return len(s) + s[0]
}

func main() {
	xs := []int{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
	total := 0
	for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
		total = total + take(xs)
	}
	sink = total + xs[0]
}
